\details{
The Yeo-Johnson transformation (Yeo and Johnson, 2000) of a value
\eqn{x \ge 0} is
\deqn{\psi_\lambda(x) = \frac{(1 + x)^\lambda - 1}{\lambda}}
(\eqn{\log(1 + x)} for \eqn{\lambda = 0}), and of a value \eqn{x < 0}
\deqn{\psi_\lambda(x) = -\frac{(1 - x)^{2 - \lambda} - 1}{2 - \lambda}}
(\eqn{-\log(1 - x)} for \eqn{\lambda = 2}). \eqn{\lambda = 1} leaves the
values unchanged; a \eqn{\lambda} below 1 compresses the upper tail, for
right-skewed variables, and a \eqn{\lambda} above 1 compresses the lower
tail, for left-skewed ones.

Each variable is first centered by its median and divided by its MAD, so
that \eqn{\lambda} does not depend on its location or units. It describes
the shape of the distribution on this robust standard scale, and cannot
be compared with the \eqn{\lambda} of \code{\link[=robust_boxcox]{robust_boxcox()}}: on the same data,
it is often much closer to 1.

It suits the variables with zero or negative values, such as
baseline-corrected, derivative or SNV-scaled spectra. Strictly positive
variables, such as raw intensities or contents, can be transformed with
either transformation.
}
\section{Robust fit}{

The parameter \eqn{\lambda} of each variable is fitted as follows:
\enumerate{
\item The variable is pre-standardized (see Details).
\item An initial \eqn{\lambda} (between -4 and 6) minimizes a robust
distance (Tukey's biweight) between the sorted, robustly standardized
transformed values and the quantiles of the normal distribution. For
this step, the transformation is continued linearly on the side that
it compresses (the upper side for \eqn{\lambda < 1}, the lower side
for \eqn{\lambda > 1}), beyond the point where the transformed value
is 1.5 times that of the quartile, so that outliers on that side
cannot dictate \eqn{\lambda}.
\item The transformed values are standardized with Huber's M-estimates of
location and scale, and those farther from the center than the
\code{quantile} of the absolute value of a standard normal variable (2.58
for the default 0.99) are given weight zero (first with the rectified
transformation of step 2). \eqn{\lambda} is then re-estimated by
maximum likelihood on the others. This is repeated \code{nbsteps} times.
\item With \code{standardize = TRUE}, the transformed variable is standardized by
the mean and standard deviation of these inliers.
}

Variables with fewer than 5 values or no spread (zero MAD) are left
unchanged. Missing values are ignored by the fit, and stay missing.
